that the risk of war  in the oec area is now greater  than ever before  in the last 30 years  that's a stark warning there from  poland's foreign minister who also  chairs the 57-member osce the  organization for security and  cooperation in europe his comments  follow a series of talks this week  between russia and western diplomats on  tensions over ukraine  european union foreign and defense  ministers held their own meeting on  russia today the eu held no direct talks  with the kremlin this week but its  foreign policy chief denies that europe  is being left out of negotiations  especially with the threat of war in its  own backyard  we are in close cooperation and we are  assured that nothing will be decided nor  even negotiated with the russians  without a close coordination with europe  and without the participation of  europeans  so after a week of high level talks  between russia and the west we are clear  on two things non-starters and red lines  if the security of europe is facing its  greatest threat in 30 years what can  what will nato be able to do about it  dwe's brussels correspondent terry  schultz asks the man at the center of  this week's negotiations nato chief jens  stoltenberg  thank you very much mr secretary general  for making time for us today  so i wanted to start off with um  an assessment by putin spokesman dmitry  peskov just in the last couple of hours  that said the talks here were  unsuccessful now that's not necessarily  a bad thing for nato given what the  russians hoped to accomplish dividing  allies getting promises not to expand  things like that but what would be your  assessment of how the council went  yesterday i think it was an important  meeting  because just the fact that 30 nato  allies were able to sit down with russia  after two years with no meeting and  meetings in network share council was  important the discussions they were very  difficult we were there for four hours  but the fact that we had difficult  discussions actually for me just proves  the importance of the meeting  because we are faced with a very  critical uh  time for european security and uh i  believe that it's important to sit down  and address those issues we listened to  the russian concern we presented our  positions we are ready to engage in  dialogue russia but we'll never  compromise on core principles for  european security have you heard  anything back from them yet i i mean  it's only been 24 hours but did you when  you said that you you offered them a  series of talks about things other than  ukraine um issues of mutual concern for  the alliance and for russia would you be  willing to go into those talks without  discussing ukraine um would those be  completely irrelevant of whether there's  any progress or even any discussion on  the de-escalation over ukraine ukraine  will be part of those discussions as we  list the long range of different topics  we could discuss which are important for  european security  but for instance also arms control  limitations on the number of missiles  but this has to be reciprocal balanced  and very viable  measures to increase transparency on  military activities uh  risk reduction many other issues  and and we believe there is scope to  actually find solutions in some of these  areas but to do so we need preparations  we need to meet and we need a  good faith uh uh in addressing uh the  substance and and russia was not in a  position and i in a way i didn't expect  that either but they were not in  position yesterday in a meeting to say  we are ready to engage  but we  offer that possibility and then  they have to go back to moscow consult  and then you will get an answer for them  later on but again would they have to  discuss ukraine as well in connection  with this i mean we know that ukraine  has to be on the agenda of every  nato-russia council since 2014. if they  were not willing to de-escalate we're  not willing to take those steps would  you also be willing to enter discussions  on other subjects but but this is very  much intertwined i mean as long as soon  as we discuss european security and as  soon as we discuss risk reduction  transparency of course ukraine will be  part of those those discussions but i  think the best way now is not to conduct  diplomacy in  in media in the public it is to express  a willingness as we did in the meeting  yesterday to address a wide range of  issues uh we we stayed a ready  proposals on the table concrete  proposals on the table ready to listen  to their concerns and not ready to  compromise some core principles but  there are many other issues to discuss  and then if they reduce it done let's  sit down and then  in the meetings not in the media  start the negotiations and the talks did  the russians ask for this to be in  writing is that one of the things that  may be holding up a response we are  ready also to provide uh proposals  in writing and we have made that clear  and then there are different formats uh  of course some of these issues can be  discussed in the nato-russia council all  the issues may be negotiated bilateral  united states uh russia and we have the  strategic talks on nuclear issues  um and then we had the oc as another  forum and then we have normandy format  with germany and france is very engaged  and i would like to commend france and  germany what they do in the norman  reformer for that many different  platforms that we can address different  issues but i think the nato russia  council is important in a way to  coordinate to allies to convey a clear  united message from nato allies to to  russia and also to identify different  issues where we can work and address and  sit down and and see if we can find a  political way forward do you think  allies were satisfied with the u.s  russian bilateral talks um we're hearing  that the u.s may have brought up some  repositioning of forces in europe during  those talks and not everybody's  comfortable with that well the united  states has made very very clear that  those meetings were not negotiations  they were actually only the same as we  did here in brussels uh in the  literature council we we floated ideas  we were looking for ways and and topics  and and and and ways to organize a  process that can ensure a political uh  way forward  what we welcome is the very close  consultation between the united states  and european allies they consulted  before the meeting in geneva with russia  they consulted afterwards  and and some people have also you know  whether europe is at the table well  europe is at the table uh uh yesterday  in the meeting with the nato in the nato  russia council we had 30 you know 28  allies uh  european allies at the table so europe  is at the table that's what makes  nato unique it brings europe and north  america together also when you engage  with russia  as many times as you say that um russia  should not have a veto over certainly  expansion but other things doesn't it  feel like we are all at this moment  sitting waiting for president putin to  decide if he wants to go ahead with  talks he still does to some extent have  a say over what everyone else is doing  what the alliance is doing of course  russia decides what they do and they  have a choice either to engage in  dialogue  uh  with nato uh and uh western allies uh or  uh confrontation uh therefore we need to  be clear-eyed about the prospects of uh  that we will not have dialogue but  actually that russia once again will use  military force against ukraine but then  we have stated clearly that there will  be severe consequences for ukraine heavy  economic sanctions we will provide  support to ukraine we to also enable  them to strengthen their ability to  defend themselves and nato allies will  of course do what is necessary to make  sure that we have the necessary forces  in eastern part alliance to put to to  defend but also deter against any  aggression against the nato allies uh  